#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:53
#, no-c-format
msgid "Introduction"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:55
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Kartesio is a program for calculating best fit curves with experimental "
"points "
"using regression algorithms or neural networks. It is free and licensed under "
"the &GNU; Public License."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:60
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Kartesio has a simple interface that allows you to plot points and a curve. "
"The "
"curve is usually calculated by the program, but you can also write it by "
"yourself."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:67
#, no-c-format
msgid "Kartesio quick start guide"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:69
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"As soon as you open Kartesio, you will get a blank table and a blank plot. "
"This "
"is also the same screen you can get in every moment just clicking on "
"(<menuchoice> <guimenu>File</guimenu> <guimenuitem>New</guimenuitem> "
"</menuchoice>). You can try to best fit your experimental points with a "
"regression algorithm or a neural network, using the tools in the appropriate "
"tab. Please note that regression algorithm needs <application>"
"maxima</application> to be installed on your computer, while the neural "
"network "
"method needs ZorbaNeural."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:71
#, no-c-format
msgid "<screeninfo>Kartesio main window</screeninfo>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: index.docbook:74
#, no-c-format
msgid "<phrase>Kartesio main window</phrase>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:80
#, no-c-format
msgid "Regression"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:82
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"You should start adding your points to the table. Obviously, a bidimensional "
"point is identified with two coordinates (on X and Y axis)."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:87
#, no-c-format
msgid "<screeninfo>Adding points</screeninfo>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: index.docbook:90
#, no-c-format
msgid "<phrase>Adding points</phrase>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:94
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"In the <guilabel>Regression</guilabel> tab, you can write a generic function "
"that will be used by Kartesio as a model for the fitting curve. The function "
"must be written with the variables x and y, and with variable coefficients "
"(represented by letters). Obviously, you can also write numeric coefficients. "
"Please take note that the function must be biunique."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:97
#, no-c-format
msgid "<screeninfo>Writing a generic function</screeninfo>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: index.docbook:100
#, no-c-format
msgid "<phrase>Writing a generic function</phrase>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:104
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Clicking the <guibutton>Best fit</guibutton> button, Kartesio will start to "
"calculate coefficients for the function you wrote, trying to best fit the "
"experimental points. The final function will appear the edit box close to the "
"bottom edge of the window."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:107
#, no-c-format
msgid "<screeninfo>Best fit done</screeninfo>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: index.docbook:110
#, no-c-format
msgid "<phrase>Best fit done</phrase>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:114
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"After the fit operation, points and the curve will be automatically plotted. "
"Anyway, you may prefer to change to plotted area to see better the image. "
"This "
"can be done using the four spinboxes: <guilabel>X min</guilabel> "
"is the minimum value of X axis, and <guilabel>X max</guilabel> "
"is the maximum value. For example, if you write respectively 0 and 1, then "
"the "
"plot will start from 0 and end to 1. The same logic works for Y axis."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:117
#, no-c-format
msgid "<screeninfo>A closer look</screeninfo>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: index.docbook:120
#, no-c-format
msgid "<phrase>A closer look</phrase>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:124
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"If you change plot limits, you may need to change also the resolution: if the "
"resolution of the plot is too little, you will see every curve as a single "
"line. If the resolution is too high you will waste a lot of CPU time to draw "
"the plot."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:127
#, no-c-format
msgid "<screeninfo>Higher resolution</screeninfo>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: index.docbook:130
#, no-c-format
msgid "<phrase>Higher resolution</phrase>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:137
#, no-c-format
msgid "Neural Network"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:139
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The neural network method works like the regression one, but you can not "
"write "
"you own function: you must choose it from a list. This list, anyway, contains "
"practically every kind of function you may want (periodic functions are not "
"usable with a neural network)."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:144
#, no-c-format
msgid "<screeninfo>Choosing the correct function</screeninfo>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: index.docbook:147
#, no-c-format
msgid "<phrase>Choosing the correct function</phrase>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:151
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Usually, back propagation training is just what you need. For this reason it "
"is "
"checked by default. Just modify the number of iterations (it should not be "
"too "
"high, or the process may end up with way too strange value) and then press "
"the "
"<guibutton>Calculate</guibutton> button. Please take note that the neural "
"network, exactly as a human brain, may give you different results: if you "
"press "
"the <guibutton>Calculate</guibutton> button more than once and you will find "
"out that the network calculates every time a different best fitting curve."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:154
#, no-c-format
msgid "<screeninfo>Back propagation training</screeninfo>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: index.docbook:157
#, no-c-format
msgid "<phrase>Back propagation training</phrase>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:161
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"If you are not satisfied by the back propagation training result, you could "
"also use the genetic algorithm training. This can be done simply checking the "
"appropriate checkbox. Genetic algorithm training takes a lot more CPU "
"resources, so you better use a very low iterations number (not more than 500)."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:164
#, no-c-format
msgid "<screeninfo>Genetic algorithm training</screeninfo>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: index.docbook:167
#, no-c-format
msgid "<phrase>Genetic algorithm training</phrase>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:174
#, no-c-format
msgid "Other useful things"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:176
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Sometimes it is useful to redraw the plot. For example, it is if you have "
"manually changed the best fitting curve or if you edited some points and you "
"don't want to recalculate the fitting function. Just use the <guibutton>"
"Draw Plot</guibutton> button."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:181
#, no-c-format
msgid "<screeninfo>Draw plot</screeninfo>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: index.docbook:184
#, no-c-format
msgid "<phrase>Draw plot</phrase>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:188
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"To know how much the fitting curve is different from your experimental "
"points, "
"you can look at the root mean square error. To add it to the plot, it is "
"needed "
"to check the checkbox <guilabel>Show RMS error</guilabel>"
". Then press the <guibutton>Draw Plot</guibutton> button to redraw the plot: "
"it "
"should contain a red label with the RMS error."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:193
#, no-c-format
msgid "<screeninfo>Showing RMS error</screeninfo>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: index.docbook:196
#, no-c-format
msgid "<phrase>Showing RMS error</phrase>"
msgstr ""

